+**Komorebi P. · Foundations 0.9.0 · Audited release candidate · 9 October 2026**
+
+This volume freezes the mathematical scope at 87 propositions. It is a foundation for a family of algebraic signal models, not a claim that one implemented learner discovers every representation used in the volume. The accompanying reference laboratory implements explicit finite sectors. Production PR-LASM remains version 2.3.0.
+
+# Reading the completed argument
+
+Start with the difference between preserving an observation and retaining enough information to predict it. Sections 1–14 develop that distinction through exact atlases, packets, transfer blocks, predictive closure and memory. Sections 15–26 construct an observable linear realization under rank and coverage assumptions and control its approximations.
+
+Sections 27–55 pass to controlled probabilities. A predictive state describes future experiments, while an invariant domain gives its coordinates probability meaning. Identification from an observable table and certification of that table's fitted model remain separate requirements. Sections 56–69 extend the construction to continuous observation measures and curved matrix domains.
+
+Sections 70–90 distinguish geometry proposals from exact verification, approximation from correction, and deterministic uncertainty from statistical coverage. Sections 91–100 connect dependent observations to a covered parameter set in a restricted, fully observable nonlinear family. That last family is not the repaired operator family from the preceding example.
+
+The **Reader's Synthesis** gives a shorter mathematical route through these results. The **Proof and Evidence Register** records each proposition's assumptions, direct internal dependencies and concrete code connections. Neither document substitutes for the proofs here.
+
+## What the model specifies
+
+A signal model declares the observations it accepts, the predictive state it uses, the operators that act on that state, and the conditions under which those operators retain their meaning. When it assigns probabilities, it also declares a reference measure, a mass functional and an admissible domain. When it makes an error claim, it names the two compared objects, the norm, the horizon and the evidence supporting that comparison.
+
+The central rule is:
+
+> Preserve the observed signal. Specify the representation used for reasoning. Express transformations in that representation. Identify the defect when transformation, compression or inference fails to commute. Refine the state, retain memory or accept a quantified approximation when the defect matters.
+
+Four distinctions govern the whole volume. Lossless storage does not imply predictive sufficiency. A valid probability model need not be accurate. A fitted model's exact certificate does not establish coverage of an unknown generating process. A uniform single-time marginal bound is not a uniform bound on complete observation records.
+
+## Frozen scope and review status
+
+The numbered results are mathematical statements with written proofs. Their classical antecedents are attributed in the source lineage. The proposition count is not a novelty count. The release audit reviews the stated assumptions and the way conclusions depend on them. Finite symbolic and numerical checks exercise examples and independent certificate replays. No Lean kernel verification or independent external referee review is claimed.
+
+One statement has a boundary clarification in this edition. Proposition 72 now explicitly assumes at least one spatial coordinate. The mass-only case has no nonzero mass-preserving perturbation and is excluded. The other 86 proposition-and-proof passages are preserved byte for byte. Their upstream definitions remain part of their hypotheses.
+
+The section and proposition numbers are retained for traceability. Historical end-of-block checkpoints have been replaced by current dependency summaries. An unchanged copy of the 0.8.0 Markdown and its original audit and manifests is included under `provenance/0.8.0/`. The previously delivered predecessor archive is identified by hash, not recursively embedded again.
